        ":question/problem": "Complete the square on $y^2-7y+\\frac{61}{4}$.\n\nFill in the nonnegative numbers.\n\n$y^2-7y+\\frac{61}{4}=(y-$ {{field-1}} $)^2+$ {{field-2}}",
        ":question/worked-solution": "The half of the linear coefficient is $-\\frac{7}{2}$. **Add and subtract** its square so the expression keeps its value.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\ny^2-7y+\\frac{61}{4}&=\\underbrace{y^2-2\\cdot \\frac{7}{2}y+\\frac{49}{4}}_{\\text{perfect square}}-\\frac{49}{4}+\\frac{61}{4} \\\\\n&=\\left(y-\\frac{7}{2}\\right)^2-\\frac{49}{4}+\\frac{61}{4} \\\\\n&=\\left(y-\\frac{7}{2}\\right)^2+3.\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe minus sign inside the square produces the required linear term $-7y$.\n\nThe blanks are $\\frac{7}{2}$ and $3$; the sign before the second blank is already supplied."
